    bigIdea: 'In Kingdom Principles, the second book of his Kingdom series, Munroe takes the Kingdom apart like a civics teacher: a king who is born, not elected, whose word is law; a Lord who owns what He rules; a territory; a commonwealth in which the King provides; and a culture His citizens learn. He argues that believers who do not understand how a kingdom works will keep living as anxious owners and religious subjects instead of secure stewards and citizens.',
    inApp: 'Read the chapters of Kingdom Principles on kings and lords (pp. 82-107), territory (pp. 113-116), commonwealth and economy (pp. 53, 108) and culture (pp. 185-192). Then read Psalm 24:1, Luke 6:46 and Leviticus 25:23 and weigh it by the Word: if Jesus is King and Lord, who owns what is in your hands, and whose word settles your choices?',
